{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 24, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a prominent figure in the Crusades and a significant political leader in the Levant during the 12th and 13th centuries. His reign was marked by military campaigns diplomatic maneuvering and efforts to consolidate the power of the County of Tripoli against various enemies including the Seljuk Turks and other Crusader states.\n\n*Early Life and Accession*\n\nRaymond III was born around 1120 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Countess Maria of Antioch. He inherited the County of Tripoli upon his father's death in 1154. His early years were spent navigating the complex political landscape of the Levant which was characterized by constant warfare and shifting alliances. He faced immediate challenges from the Seljuk Sultanate of Rum a powerful Turkish empire that posed a constant threat to Crusader territories. Raymond III inherited a county weakened by previous conflicts and internal strife requiring him to quickly assert his authority and defend its borders. He focused on rebuilding the county's defenses and forging alliances with neighboring Crusader states like Antioch and Jerusalem.\n\nRaymond III engaged in numerous military campaigns throughout his reign. He fought against the Seljuks in several battles often employing a strategy of defensive warfare and raiding. He also dealt with internal rebellions and power struggles within the county striving to maintain stability and consolidate his rule. His military leadership was crucial in preserving the County of Tripoli's independence during a period of intense pressure from its enemies. He understood the importance of strong fortifications and invested heavily in strengthening the county’s castles and walls.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances*\n\nRaymond III was a skilled diplomat and understood the importance of alliances in maintaining the County of Tripoli's survival. He cultivated relationships with other Crusader states seeking mutual support against common enemies. He frequently engaged in negotiations with the Seljuk Turks attempting to secure truces and prevent large-scale wars. His diplomatic efforts were often complex and required a delicate balance of power. He was known for his pragmatic approach to foreign policy prioritizing the security of the county above ideological considerations. He engaged in alliances with various entities including the Kingdom of Sicily and the Holy Roman Empire seeking support against the Seljuks and other threats.\n\n*Legacy and Death*\n\nRaymond III died in 1199 after a long and eventful reign. He is remembered as a capable military leader a shrewd diplomat and a dedicated defender of the County of Tripoli. His efforts helped to preserve the county's independence for several decades despite facing constant pressure from its enemies. He left behind a county that was stronger and more stable than when he had inherited it. His reign is considered a high point in the history of the County of Tripoli a period of relative peace and prosperity amidst the chaos of the Crusades. He is often portrayed as a pragmatic ruler willing to make difficult choices to ensure the survival of his county a legacy that continues to be studied by historians.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.2371193766593933}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.20648594200611115}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.054002758115530014}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.00023135847004596144}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.5987286790041253e-05}, {"t